\title{Fongoro Language Resources for K-12 Curriculum Development}
\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Fongoro (ISO 639-3: fgr; also Gele, Kole) is a severely endangered language of eastern Chad (Ouadda\"i region, Sila department, Fongoro canton, along the Sudan border), described as Central Sudanic of uncertain classification. Sources report only a few elderly speakers. Glottolog argues that the available lexical data show it to be a dialectal variant of Gula (kcm). Very little exists beyond brief reference entries, and nothing suitable for schools was found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school materials in or for Fongoro were found in the searches.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Fongoro language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fongoro_language}
\item Glottolog entry (Gele/Dar Fongoro, with the dialect-of-Gula argument): \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/iso/fgr}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry (cites Dimmendaal and Voeltz 2007): \url{https://endangeredlanguages.com/lang/570}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project profile of the Fongoro in Chad (notes shift toward Fur, Arabic and other regional languages): \url{https://www.joshuaproject.net/index.php/people_groups/11219/CD}
\end{itemize}
No scripture translation was found in the search results.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Global Recordings Network language page for fgr (listing only; contents not verified): \url{https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/fgr}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
With only a few elderly speakers and an unsettled classification, a school curriculum in Fongoro is not currently feasible from published sources. Any effort would begin with documentation work with remaining speakers and with the Gula question. Regional languages (Arabic, Fur) are the practical media of instruction.
\end{document}
